% ============================================================================
% quadrilateral-types — five common quadrilaterals and the ONE defining
% property each one's source states (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A four-sided polygon (a quadrilateral) comes in named families, and each
% family is pinned down by ONE defining property: a rectangle is the one with
% four right angles, a rhombus is the one whose sides are all the same length,
% a parallelogram is the one with opposite sides parallel, a trapezoid is the
% one with (just) two sides parallel, and a square is the one whose equal-length
% sides sit at right angles to each other. Before you can reason about a
% quadrilateral — its area formula, its symmetry, whether it tiles — you must
% first know WHICH named family it is. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? quadrilateral_property(rhombus, $Property)   % all_sides_same_length
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `quadrilateral_property(shape, property)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the property WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these five quadrilaterals (it never
% invents a property).
%
% Each quadrilateral and the single defining property its source states, as a
% little truth table:
%
%     quadrilateral   defining property (source's word)   a beginner's cue
%     -------------   ---------------------------------   -----------------------
%     square          equal_sides_at_right_angles         equal sides, all corners square
%     rectangle       four_right_angles                   four square corners
%     rhombus         all_sides_same_length               a "pushed-over" square
%     parallelogram   opposite_sides_parallel             both pairs of sides parallel
%     trapezoid       two_sides_parallel                  exactly one pair parallel
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a property and recall its quadrilateral:
%
%     ? quadrilateral_property($Shape, four_right_angles)   % rectangle
%
% A word that is NOT one of these five quadrilaterals — `triangle`, `pentagon`,
% `circle` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a property. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table
% safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the quadrilateral→property
% pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `property` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% defining property the source EXPLICITLY states for that quadrilateral in its
% opening definition — never one the source does not support. Each value is
% distinct, so no two families collapse to the same defining property. Where the
% source phrases a family two ways (a rhombus is "equilateral" AND has "all
% sides the same length"), the atom uses the source's own descriptive words, so
% every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every quadrilateral→
% property pair below was read out of a fetched Wolfram MathWorld definition
% page, and any pair whose property could not be verified there would have been
% dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the
% verbatim opening-definition span that states it:
%
%   square → equal_sides_at_right_angles
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Square.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — Weisstein, Eric W.)
%     "The term 'square' can be used to mean either a square number or a
%      geometric figure consisting of a convex quadrilateral with sides of equal
%      length that are positioned at right angles to each other as illustrated
%      above."
%
%   rectangle → four_right_angles
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Rectangle.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — Weisstein, Eric W.)
%     "A closed planar quadrilateral with opposite sides of equal lengths a and
%      b, and with four right angles."
%
%   rhombus → all_sides_same_length
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Rhombus.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — Weisstein, Eric W.)
%     "A rhombus is a quadrilateral with both pairs of opposite sides parallel
%      and all sides the same length, i.e., an equilateral parallelogram."
%
%   parallelogram → opposite_sides_parallel
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Parallelogram.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — Weisstein, Eric W.)
%     "A parallelogram is a quadrilateral with opposite sides parallel (and
%      therefore opposite angles equal)."
%
%   trapezoid → two_sides_parallel
%     https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Trapezoid.html
%     (Wolfram MathWorld — Weisstein, Eric W.)
%     "A trapezoid is a quadrilateral with two sides parallel."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the MathWorld statement that
% fixes the first row (square → equal_sides_at_right_angles). Every OTHER row's
% per-fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each property remains
% independently checkable. All sources are Wolfram MathWorld, a primary
% mathematics reference (the same source the geometry/shapes and geometry/
% solid-shapes libraries cite at `authoritative`), so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table quadrilateral_property {
    columns shape, property

    row (square, equal_sides_at_right_angles)
    row (rectangle, four_right_angles)
    row (rhombus, all_sides_same_length)
    row (parallelogram, opposite_sides_parallel)
    row (trapezoid, two_sides_parallel)

    source "The term 'square' can be used to mean either a square number or a geometric figure consisting of a convex quadrilateral with sides of equal length that are positioned at right angles to each other as illustrated above."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Square.html"
    trust authoritative
}
